                <pb facs="0355" n="347" sameAs="frus:frus1938v01/pg347"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00111 Armament Control/1716: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bowers</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">Then in France, to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">St.
                                Jean de Luz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-25T19:00:00+00:00">March 25, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 25—4:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>468. Press report on our policy reading “Loyalist Spain will continue to be
                    denied arms from America while the rebels still receive them from Germany and
                    Italy who are both free to buy from America” echoes complaints that have been
                    made to me. Practically certain if Germany and Italy are buying war material in
                    America it is for use in Spain against which both are waging war. If our records
                    show Germany and Italy are not receiving war material from us I could use the
                    fact discreetly and effectively with our more important critics here.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
